From: Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 00:38:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: tell a wrong key from a damaged page in obfsvfs

Each sealed page now carries, in the 4 reserved bytes after its nonce,
a check of its database's key: an HMAC-SHA-256 of a label of its own
under the key, from NSS, with its last bit set so that no check is
zero, as those bytes are in a page sealed before it. It tells nothing
of the key, and tells what a tag cannot: why a page does not open.

A database's open reads its pages and its log's as they are on disk,
before SQLite reads any. Page 1 opening under the key, or any page
carrying its check, shows the key to be the database's own. Where none
does and some page carries another check, the key is not the
database's: the open is refused, SQLITE_AUTH, before anything of it is
opened or made, and nothing takes that for damage. Under its own key a
page that does not open stays an I/O error, SQLITE_IOERR_DATA.

Fixed with it: a frame of the log that did not open was passed to
SQLite as torn only after another frame had opened, so a log whose
first frame a crash tore, the first commit after a checkpoint, made
the whole database an I/O error. Under a key shown to be its own, a
torn frame now ends the log there, as SQLite in the clear does. A
database sealed before the checks opens as it did.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
